# Introduction to Dynamic Programming {#sec-dpi}

::: {.callout-note title="Sources"}
**`dp_book` ch. 1** (seven lessons, five patterns, toolkit) · **`dp_ladder`** part I (*First Transitions*: families `linear`, `house`, `grid`, `machine`, `kadane`) · **CLRS** ch. 14 (dynamic programming) · **Erickson** *Algorithms* ch. 3 · **CPH** ch. 7 · AtCoder Educational DP Contest (tasks A, C).

**Coverage:** what DP is (overlapping subproblems, optimal substructure) · recursion → memoisation → table → O(1) space · the five-question recipe (state, transition, base cases, answer, order) and the cost check · counting vs optimising vs deciding, ordered vs unordered counting, counting mod p, "impossible" values · top-down vs bottom-up, pull vs push, rolling arrays · recognising DP from the statement and the constraints · reconstruction · debugging and stress testing DP · five patterns: linear recurrences, include/exclude, grid paths, multi-state, Kadane with extra states.
:::

::: {.callout-tip title="What you will be able to do"}
- Turn a brute-force recursion into a memoised recursion, a table, and an O(1)-space loop, and say what each step costs.
- Answer the five questions (state, transition, base cases, answer, order) for a new problem before writing code.
- Tell counting, optimisation and feasibility versions of a problem apart, and avoid the ordered-vs-unordered counting trap.
- Add a state dimension (last choice, holding, phase, deleted) instead of looking for a new algorithm.
- Rebuild the actual solution (the path, the chosen items) from a filled table.
- Find DP bugs quickly by printing the table and stress testing against a brute force.
:::


## Why this matters

Dynamic programming (DP) is one of the most frequent topics in algorithm interviews, and `dp` is one of the most common problem tags on Codeforces. It also scares people, because DP solutions look like magic: a two-line recurrence solves a problem that seemed to need exponential search.

The magic is a habit. You describe a **subproblem** precisely, write how its answer depends on **smaller** subproblems, and store each answer once. This chapter builds that habit on small problems, so that the five questions become automatic. Every later DP chapter — knapsack, intervals, bitmasks, trees, the optimisations — uses the same five questions with a bigger state.

## Lesson 1: what dynamic programming is {#sec-dpi-what}

A problem is a good DP candidate when it has two properties:

1. **Optimal substructure.** The answer to the problem can be built from answers to smaller instances of the same problem. (For optimisation problems this is the property from @sec-pf-induction: an optimal solution contains optimal solutions to its subproblems.)
2. **Overlapping subproblems.** A plain recursion reaches the *same* smaller instance many times.

The first property makes a recursion correct. The second makes it slow, and DP removes the slowness by computing each distinct subproblem **once**. Naive Fibonacci makes $2F(n+1) - 1$ calls for only $n + 1$ distinct arguments; @fig-cx-fib-tree in the complexity chapter shows the repeated subtrees.

Without overlap there is nothing to save: merge sort also builds the answer from smaller instances, but its two halves never share work, so it is divide and conquer, not DP.

### The subproblem graph

Draw one node per distinct subproblem and an arrow from each subproblem to every subproblem that uses its answer. For DP to work this graph must be a **DAG**: no subproblem may depend, directly or indirectly, on itself. The DP computes the nodes in a topological order, and its running time is the sum over nodes of the work done at that node.

::: {#fig-dpi-fib-dag}

