## Lesson 2: from recursion to a loop {#sec-dpi-fib}

Every DP in this book can be written in four forms. Learn to move between them; each removes one kind of waste.

| Form | How | Time | Extra space |
|---|---|---|---|
| Plain recursion | the recurrence, called directly | exponential | $O(n)$ stack |
| Memoisation (top-down) | recursion + a cache checked first | $O(\text{states} \times \text{transitions})$ | table + stack |
| Tabulation (bottom-up) | fill a table in dependency order | same | table |
| Rolling variables | keep only the cells the next step reads | same | $O(\text{window})$ |

::: {#dpi-fib-forms .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
calls = {"plain": 0, "memo": 0}

def fib_plain(n):
    calls["plain"] += 1
    return n if n < 2 else fib_plain(n - 1) + fib_plain(n - 2)

def fib_memo(n, cache=None):
    cache = {} if cache is None else cache
    calls["memo"] += 1
    if n < 2:
        return n
    if n not in cache:
        cache[n] = fib_memo(n - 1, cache) + fib_memo(n - 2, cache)
    return cache[n]

def fib_table(n):
    dp = [0] * (n + 1)
    if n:
        dp[1] = 1
    for i in range(2, n + 1):
        dp[i] = dp[i - 1] + dp[i - 2]
    return dp[n]

def fib_rolling(n):
    a, b = 0, 1                      # F(i), F(i+1)
    for _ in range(n):
        a, b = b, a + b
    return a

for n in range(0, 25):
    calls = {"plain": 0, "memo": 0}
    vals = {fib_plain(n), fib_memo(n), fib_table(n), fib_rolling(n)}
    assert len(vals) == 1
    assert calls["memo"] <= 2 * n + 1          # each n computed once, each looked up at most twice
print("calls for n = 24:", calls)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
calls for n = 24: {'plain': 150049, 'memo': 47}
```
:::
:::


The memoised version is the recursion you already wrote, plus two lines. That is why the usual workflow is: **write the recursion first, check it on small inputs, then add memoisation, then convert to a table only if you need speed or less stack.**

