### Problem: Domino and tromino tiling {#sec-dpi-p1-tiling}

::: {.p-statement}
Count the ways to tile a $2 \times n$ board ($1 \le n \le 1000$) with $2 \times 1$ dominoes and L-shaped trominoes (both may be rotated), modulo $10^9 + 7$.

Source: [LC 790 · Domino and Tromino Tiling](https://leetcode.com/problems/domino-and-tromino-tiling/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
Fill the board column by column. A tromino leaves the next column **half** filled, so "the first $i$ columns are full" is not enough as a state. Add a second state: the first $i$ columns are full **and one cell of column $i+1$ sticks out**. Two coupled recurrences then collapse into one.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
- **States.** $f[i]$ = tilings of a full $2 \times i$ board. $g[i]$ = tilings of a $2 \times i$ board plus one extra cell in column $i+1$ (top or bottom; by symmetry both give the same count).
- **Transitions.** Look at how the rightmost column is completed:
  - $f[i] = f[i-1]$ (vertical domino) $+ f[i-2]$ (two horizontal dominoes) $+ 2\,g[i-2]$ (a tromino finishing a sticking-out board, two mirror images);
  - $g[i] = g[i-1]$ (a horizontal domino extends the overhang) $+ f[i-1]$ (a tromino creates it).
- **Base cases.** $f[0] = 1$, $f[1] = 1$, $g[0] = 0$, $g[1] = 1$. **Answer.** $f[n]$.
- **Closed form.** Eliminating $g$ gives $f[n] = 2f[n-1] + f[n-3]$.
:::

