$f$ is a full board, $g$ is a board with one cell sticking out, for width $0$ through $5$. Step 1 is the two base widths. Step 2 reaches width $3$, where $f = 5$, the five pictures above. Step 3 reaches width $5$, where $f = 24$. Each new $f$ reads the two earlier full boards and the overhang from two columns back.

:::

::: {.callout-important title="Why the optimal recurrence is $f[n] = 2f[n-1] + f[n-3]$"}
The two-state recurrence is already $O(n)$, but it carries an auxiliary sequence $g$ whose only job is to remember a half-filled column. Eliminating $g$ leaves one sequence, and then three rolling numbers.

For $i \ge 2$, with $g[0] = 0$:

$$
f[i] = f[i-1] + f[i-2] + 2g[i-2], \qquad g[i] = g[i-1] + f[i-1].
$$

From the second equation, $g[i-2] = g[i-1] - f[i-2]$. Substitute:

$$
f[i] = f[i-1] + f[i-2] + 2(g[i-1] - f[i-2]) = f[i-1] - f[i-2] + 2g[i-1].
$$

The first equation one step later says $2g[i-1] = f[i+1] - f[i] - f[i-1]$. Put that in:

$$
f[i] = f[i-1] - f[i-2] + f[i+1] - f[i] - f[i-1],
$$

which rearranges to $f[i+1] = 2f[i] + f[i-2]$. Shift the index by one:

$$
f[n] = 2f[n-1] + f[n-3] \qquad (n \ge 3),
$$

with $f[0] = 1$, $f[1] = 1$, $f[2] = 2$. Only three earlier values are read, so the loop keeps `a, b, c` and replaces them by `b, c, (2c + a) mod M`. That is $O(n)$ time and $O(1)$ memory.

A $3\times 3$ companion matrix,

$$
\begin{pmatrix} f[n] \\ f[n-1] \\ f[n-2] \end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix} 2 & 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}^{n-2}
\begin{pmatrix} f[2] \\ f[1] \\ f[0] \end{pmatrix},
$$

computes the same value in $O(\log n)$ multiplications. For $n \le 1000$ the three variables are what you submit; the matrix is the same algorithm when $n$ is huge. Reduce modulo $10^9+7$ on every assignment: $f[1000]$ has hundreds of digits.

In the two-state loop, $f[i]$ must be computed from the **old** $g[i-2]$ before $g$ is overwritten. The closed form has no $g$, so that bug cannot happen.
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

::: {.panel-tabset .solution-forms}

#### Recursion

::: {.form-next}
`f(i)` is the number of ways to tile a full `2×i` board. `g(i)` is the number of ways to tile that board with one extra cell sticking into the next column. A full board ends with a vertical domino (`f(i-1)`), two horizontal dominoes (`f(i-2)`), or a tromino closing a sticking-out board (`2·g(i-2)`). The overhang itself is a tromino on a full board (`f(i-1)`) or a domino extending an overhang (`g(i-1)`). `f(0) = f(1) = 1` and `g(1) = 1`. Calling `f` and `g` straight from these formulas is the recursion. It is exponential, because each call repeats smaller lengths.
:::

