## Pattern 3: grid paths {#sec-dpi-p3}

**From one index to two.** Every DP so far walked along a line. A grid has two coordinates, but the question that gives the transition is unchanged — *what was the last move?* — and the answer is "from above or from the left". The fill order just has to finish both neighbours first; anti-diagonals (below) make that visible.


**Recognise it.** A grid, moves only right and down (or any moves that never return to an earlier cell), and a question about paths from one corner to the other: count them, or find the cheapest.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
$dp[r][c]$ = answer for paths from the start to cell $(r, c)$. The last move came from above or from the left: $dp[r][c] = \text{combine}(dp[r-1][c],\ dp[r][c-1]) \oplus \text{cell}(r, c)$. Fill row by row (each row left to right). Only the previous row is ever read, so one rolling row of length $m$ is enough.
:::

**Complexity.** $O(nm)$ time, $O(m)$ space.

