### Problem: Unique paths II {#sec-dpi-p3-unique}

::: {.p-statement}
A robot moves only right or down on an $m \times n$ grid ($1 \le m, n \le 100$) from the top-left to the bottom-right cell. Some cells are obstacles (1). Count the paths that avoid obstacles. (The answer fits in a signed 32-bit integer.)

Source: [LC 63 · Unique Paths II](https://leetcode.com/problems/unique-paths-ii/){.solved difficulty="Medium"}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
Every path into $(r, c)$ arrives from $(r-1, c)$ or from $(r, c-1)$, and those two groups are disjoint, so the counts add. An obstacle cell has 0 paths into it, which automatically blocks everything that would pass through it.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
- **State.** $dp[r][c]$ = number of obstacle-free paths from $(0,0)$ to $(r,c)$.
- **Transition.** 0 on an obstacle; otherwise $dp[r-1][c] + dp[r][c-1]$ (missing neighbours count as 0).
- **Base case.** $dp[0][0] = 1$ if the start is free, else 0. **Answer.** $dp[m-1][n-1]$.
- **Rolling row.** Keep one array `row`; `row[c] += row[c-1]` updates "from above" (the old value) plus "from the left" (the new value).
:::

