::: {.callout-important title="Why one rolling row is enough, and why it is scanned left to right"}
Cell $(r, c)$ reads $(r-1, c)$ and $(r, c-1)$. In row-major order, $(r-1, c)$ is the value still sitting in `row[c]` from the previous row, and $(r, c-1)$ is the value just written into `row[c-1]`. One array plays both roles:

```
if obstacle: row[c] = 0          # nothing passes through here
elif c > 0:  row[c] += row[c-1]  # old row[c] (above) + new row[c-1] (left)
```

Scanning **right to left** would make `row[c-1]` still the previous row, so you would add the wrong neighbour. An obstacle must **write 0**, not skip the cell: a skipped cell would keep the path count from the previous row and leak paths through the wall.

`row[0]` starts at 1, meaning "one way to be at the start before any row is processed". If the start cell is an obstacle, the first assignment zeroes it and the answer stays 0. This is $O(mn)$ time and $O(n)$ memory. You cannot do better in the worst case: every cell can change the answer, so you read the whole grid, and the answer is one integer.
:::

The buttons are in order. Each one keeps the previous solution and changes only what that form changes.

