##### Python

::: {#dpi-unique .cell execution_count=72}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def unique_rec(grid):
    """Recursion. Ways from (r, c) to the goal: step down, or step right."""
    R, C = len(grid), len(grid[0])

    def go(r, c):
        if r >= R or c >= C or grid[r][c]:
            return 0
        if (r, c) == (R - 1, C - 1):
            return 1
        return go(r + 1, c) + go(r, c + 1)

    return go(0, 0)
```
:::


##### C++

```cpp
int uniRec(int r, int c, const vector<vector<int>>& g) {
    int R = g.size(), C = g[0].size();
    if (r >= R || c >= C || g[r][c]) return 0;
    if (r == R - 1 && c == C - 1) return 1;
    return uniRec(r + 1, c, g) + uniRec(r, c + 1, g);
}
```
:::

#### Memo

::: {.form-next}
The recursion is the right count, and `(r, c)` is entered once per path that reaches it. There are only `mn` cells. Store `memo[r][c]` the first time `go(r, c)` returns, using `-1` for "not yet" because a real answer can be 0 when the goal is walled off. The body is the recursion from the previous button. After this, each cell is computed once.
