## Pattern 4: multi-state DP {#sec-dpi-p4}

**From where we are to what just happened.** In every problem so far the position alone decided the future. As soon as *yesterday's choice* restricts today's — the same activity twice, buying right after selling — the position is not enough. Do not look for a new algorithm: **add that fact to the state**.


**Recognise it.** A linear DP where the best choice at step $i$ depends on **something about the previous step**: which activity you did yesterday, whether you hold a stock, whether you just sold, how many transactions are used. Instead of looking for a new algorithm, **add that something to the state**.

::: {.callout-important title="Template"}
$dp[i][s]$ = best value after step $i$, ending in state $s$ (from a small set $S$). Draw the allowed state changes as a **state machine** (nodes = states, edges = moves with their gain); each edge becomes one term of a transition. The answer is the best $dp[n][s]$ over the allowed final states.
:::

**Complexity.** $O(n \cdot |S| \cdot \text{edges per state})$ time, $O(|S|)$ space with rolling rows.

### Problem: Vacation {#sec-dpi-p4-vacation}

::: {.p-statement}
Over $N$ days ($1 \le N \le 10^5$), Taro does one of three activities A, B, C each day, gaining $a_i$, $b_i$ or $c_i$ happiness ($\le 10^4$). He may not do the same activity on two consecutive days. Maximise total happiness.

Source: [AC dp_c · Vacation](https://atcoder.jp/contests/dp/tasks/dp_c){.solved}
:::

::: {.p-intuition}
"Best total up to day $i$" is not enough: tomorrow's options depend on today's activity. Add it to the state: $dp[i][j]$ = best total for days $1..i$ when day $i$'s activity is $j$.
:::

::: {.p-approach}
- **State.** $dp[i][j]$, $j \in \{A, B, C\}$. **Transition.** $dp[i][j] = \text{gain}_i(j) + \max_{k \ne j} dp[i-1][k]$.
- **Base.** $dp[1][j] = \text{gain}_1(j)$. **Answer.** $\max_j dp[N][j]$.
- Three numbers per day, so $O(1)$ space with rolling variables.
:::

